k and  in many of the mice it does not  through some phenotypic analysis it  appears that cd40 agonist promotes uh  what we're calling in this in this  publication a short-lived effector  t-cell phenotype  so in the blue  and the blue dots for cd48 in a single  therapy at day 14 we're seeing a lot of  those klrg1 positive cells  we're seeing  we're seeing a decrease in tox  expression we're seeing a decrease in  pd1 expression and we're seeing a  decrease in leg 3 expression on our  tetramer binding cells so again remember  the combination of pd1 and leg 3 is our  exhaustion phenotype  and we're seeing essentially fewer of  those and more of these klrg1 positive  cells  earlier  consistent with a really strong response  really quick and you just get rid of the  tumor  at later time points  like three months after tumor cell  injection when the mice have had no  luminescence for two months or so  we see  tetramer binding cells not only in the  pancreas and the spleen which you would  expect but also in sites of potential  tumor metastasis the liver in the lung  shown on the left we had over ten  percent of the cd8 t cells  by flow cytometry in those locations um  found the tetramer  but interestingly  um in the pancreas is where our tetramer  binding cells express the residency  markers cd49a and cd103  in addition if we re-challenge these  mice that have cured a previous tumor  with a second tumor in the pancreas  a hundred days after the first tumor um  the tetramer binding cells expanded  10-fold to 100-fold in the blood the  spleen in the pancreas consistent with  really robust t-cell memory responses  uh two two straightforward things that  uh classically trained immunologists  would ask in this setting are is what  what are  what effector molecules is this response  dependent on and the first two  that came to mind for us were interferon  gamma and tnf alpha because those are  the those are the cytokines that we look  for in our peptide restimulation  experimen